import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.uPk2SrVb.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"2026-10-29 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"Verifiable claims, execution and credential ownership, recovery gaps, durable idempotency and release boundaries.","frontmatter":{"title":"2026-10-29 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"Verifiable claims, execution and credential ownership, recovery gaps, durable idempotency and release boundaries."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-29.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-29.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-29.md"};function s(o,e,c,l,d,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="_2026-10-29-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" tabindex="-1">2026-10-29 Agent Research and Product Intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-10-29-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2026-10-29 Agent Research and Product Intelligence&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The central conclusion is that <strong>success claims need receipts and state evidence, pooled/recovered execution must preserve authority owners, and history cleanup must not recycle old operation identity</strong>. Zhao&#39;s newly curated resources, Codex chat-specific MCP leases, Paperclip recovered writes and Beads permanent tombstones converge on these issues. OpenHands SDK and Orca advance official delivery, while AutoGen adds an explicit end-of-maintenance notice.</p><p>Actual run start: 2026-10-29 12:47:01 Asia/Shanghai. The main incremental window starts at the previous successful check start, October 28 11:18:02, and ends at this start. Source checkpoints and published reports are used for deduplication; partial feeds retain older pending windows. Historical papers are labeled newly curated with original dates. All CodeFlowMu/FCoP relationships are <strong>pending validation leads</strong>, not confirmed local defects, completed experiments or development assignments.</p><h2 id="_1-yue-zhao-curates-claim-verification-typed-protocols-and-coding-agent-safety" tabindex="-1">1. Yue Zhao curates claim verification, typed protocols and coding-agent safety <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-yue-zhao-curates-claim-verification-typed-protocols-and-coding-agent-safety" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Yue Zhao curates claim verification, typed protocols and coding-agent safety&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：NEW CURATION / COMMITTED; PAPERS PREDATE THIS WINDOW</strong></p><p>This window&#39;s awesome-auditable-ai commits add Concordat, V³AST-Bench and agent-claimcheck and incorporate maintainer-reviewed SciΠvot. Zhao is the curator, not automatically the author of these works. Concordat was submitted October 27 and enforces a finite-state protocol before effects; on 50 airline tasks the standard gpt-4.1 agent made unconfirmed changes in 13 tasks versus zero for the constrained agent. V³AST-Bench, submitted October 26, pairs 212 risk tasks with capability twins to separate harm avoidance from incapability. SciΠvot, submitted October 15, contains 248 selected human-correction cases; its below-chance model results are not general scientific-task performance.</p><p>agent-claimcheck checks success claims against receipts and caller-supplied probes; its rule-path receipt_only score of 0.70 is below the 0.80 verified threshold. Its 300 traces are synthetic and co-designed with the rules, not evidence of real-world error rates. A new agent-config writing handoff pins sources, avoids global configuration changes and separates style checks from factual review.. Pending FCoP lead: completion, authorization compliance and capability need separate evidence. Interaction: independent traces, probe trust and selection bias.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/commit/4583670fdbe6aafd98543d48b4a3f26fda1e68f8" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37461" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35188" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21082" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a> · <a href="https://github.com/B0yko/agent-claimcheck" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 5</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/agent-config/blob/6a375896f210542d622e1bcc4ba58e8dfde7a286/docs/writing-harness-handoff.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 6</a></p><h2 id="_2-autogen-merges-end-of-maintenance-notice-while-github-archived-remains-false" tabindex="-1">2. AutoGen merges end-of-maintenance notice while GitHub archived remains false <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-autogen-merges-end-of-maintenance-notice-while-github-archived-remains-false" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. AutoGen merges end-of-maintenance notice while GitHub archived remains false&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MAINTENANCE NOTICE COMMITTED; REPOSITORY archived=false</strong></p><p>Commit d07c667 adds an official documentation caution that Microsoft no longer provides bug/security fixes, features or support and directs new users to Microsoft Agent Framework and existing users to a migration guide. The notice describes the project as archived/no longer maintained, while the repository API reports archived=false. That does not establish a read-only repository, nor does the API flag invalidate the maintenance notice.</p><p>Pending lead: framework/dependency decisions require maintenance commitments, not stars or one API flag. Retain the original research object and history. Interaction: session, tool, approval and persisted-state compatibility during migration. No migration was performed locally.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/commit/d07c667a3239ea4ab7af62a53b0f4ff205a1f23d" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_3-codex-main-adds-chat-leases-to-pooled-mcp-connections-assessments-do-not-authorize" tabindex="-1">3. Codex main adds chat leases to pooled MCP connections; assessments do not authorize <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-codex-main-adds-chat-leases-to-pooled-mcp-connections-assessments-do-not-authorize" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Codex main adds chat leases to pooled MCP connections; assessments do not authorize&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #57978/#57757; STABLE INCLUSION NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#57978 opens one host-owned lease per chat on pooled stdio servers advertising codex/connection-lease, overriding caller-supplied lease metadata. Pending cancellations precede release; failed/timed-out cleanup removes the connection from the pool. Unsupported servers retain existing behavior. #57757 delivers typed Guardian assessments over SSE/WebSocket with response/item identifiers and requires_review, ignoring malformed assessments. Assessments stay out of history and do not authorize execution. Stable remains 0.165.0; alpha releases do not establish stable inclusion.</p><p>Pending CodeFlowMu lead: connection pooling must preserve chat ownership and observation is not authority. Interaction: sibling-chat survival, cancellation/release ordering and authorization when assessment delivery is missing.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57978" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57757" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_4-openai-agents-js-delegates-sandbox-environment-preparation-to-clients" tabindex="-1">4. OpenAI Agents JS delegates sandbox environment preparation to clients <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-openai-agents-js-delegates-sandbox-environment-preparation-to-clients" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. OpenAI Agents JS delegates sandbox environment preparation to clients&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #2085; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#2085 adds SandboxClient.environmentPreparation=&#39;client&#39; across create, explicit/RunState resume and preserved-session reuse. The runner retains unresolved references so Temporal workflows need not access worker allowlists or resolved secrets; trusted mount/path policies remain authoritative and provider-state identity is preserved. Version-6 sandbox envelopes carry ownership, v5 remains readable and RunState uses schema 1.24 when v6 is present. Resume and final authority binding share a state transaction.</p><p>Pending lead: orchestration, execution and secret resolution are distinct roles; resume must not silently transfer preparation authority. Interaction: v5/v6 reserialization, failed-resume rollback and cross-client ownership checks.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/pull/2085" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_5-openhands-sdk-1-59-0-releases-budgets-recovery-and-masking-fixes" tabindex="-1">5. OpenHands SDK 1.59.0 releases budgets, recovery and masking fixes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-openhands-sdk-1-59-0-releases-budgets-recovery-and-masking-fixes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. OpenHands SDK 1.59.0 releases budgets, recovery and masking fixes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED; STATE ADVANCE FROM PREVIOUS MERGES</strong></p><p>Version 1.59.0 explicitly includes #6257/#6279 conversation budgets/client visibility, #6197 tolerant damaged-log loading, #6155/#6259 ID/timestamp masking protection and replayed OAuth callbacks returning stored job status. This advances several earlier merged/unreleased findings to an official version. Budgets still check accumulated conversation cost between steps and can overshoot by one model call; tolerant loading does not establish completeness. Enyst directly contributes budget, OAuth and child-conversation work.</p><p>Pending lead: an official version is now available as a reproduction baseline, not proof of local acceptance. Interaction: exposing history-gap warnings to verifiers and displaying accumulated/child-agent spend.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/releases/tag/v1.59.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_6-openhands-canvas-binds-secret-consent-to-app-revisions-and-exact-names" tabindex="-1">6. OpenHands Canvas binds secret consent to app revisions and exact names <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-openhands-canvas-binds-secret-consent-to-app-revisions-and-exact-names" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. OpenHands Canvas binds secret consent to app revisions and exact names&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED SDK #6041; CANVAS #18572 OPEN; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#6041 lets managed App backends declare secret names and binds consent to the installed revision and exact names; either change requires fresh consent. Values are resolved from encrypted storage only at startup, not placed in manifests/browser bundles. Enablement prepares, starts and waits for health; failures remain disabled and revocation clears consent/stops the app. The merge postdates 1.59.0, so inclusion is not claimed. Companion Canvas #18572 is OPEN and full frontend delivery is unverified. Upstream human integration evidence is not local/exhaustive security acceptance.</p><p>Pending lead: installation, page mounting, execution and secret authority are separate facts; approvals need version identity. Interaction: revision/revocation/crash-restart races and streaming-redaction limits.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6041" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/pull/18572" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_7-paperclip-merges-transcript-recovery-account-bound-writes-and-bounded-readiness" tabindex="-1">7. Paperclip merges transcript recovery, account-bound writes and bounded readiness <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-paperclip-merges-transcript-recovery-account-bound-writes-and-bounded-readiness" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Paperclip merges transcript recovery, account-bound writes and bounded readiness&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #17456/#17960/#18037; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>Previously OPEN #17456 is merged: visible-view interests share a connection, subscription precedes catch-up and sequence/log identity deduplicates output. Delivery is capped at 1,000 items/1 MiB; overflow resynchronizes and interest does not alter execution, ACKs, checkpoints or authority. #17960 binds recovered writes to the original account, task and key, automatically retrying only server-idempotent operations and reconciling uncertain receipts; account changes cancel old work. #18037 adds bounded administrator upgradeReadiness observations that cannot be positive with missing ownership/checkpoints or incomplete reads. It does not complete cross-artifact qualification.</p><p>High learning value and competitive risk; increase main-radar recovery/evidence-visibility attention. Pending FCoP lead: reconnection, durable writes and takeover eligibility are distinct. Interaction: catch-up after revocation, receipt/retry races and pending/conflict/manual-retry convergence.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17456" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17960" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/18037" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_8-paperclip-adds-default-off-coordinator-linking-conversations-tasks-and-decisions" tabindex="-1">8. Paperclip adds default-off Coordinator linking conversations, tasks and decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-paperclip-adds-default-off-coordinator-linking-conversations-tasks-and-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. Paperclip adds default-off Coordinator linking conversations, tasks and decisions&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #17983; EXPERIMENT DEFAULT OFF; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#17983 adds a personal opt-in Coordinator workspace without replacing classic. Agent conversations link task proposals/creation back to source context. Questions, decision receipts, exact review targets and history reuse canonical task/interaction records in a Needs You queue, preserving access, assignment, revision binding and output-review gates. API merged_at confirms merging despite stale unmerged prose in the description. No new live-provider acceptance is established here.</p><p>Medium-high learning value and competitive risk; increase main-radar employee/decision-model attention. Pending lead: cross-surface decisions need one factual owner rather than duplicate button state. Interaction: stale review cards after revision changes and per-account preference isolation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17983" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_9-orca-1-4-243-244-ships-host-changes-with-explicit-live-process-boundaries" tabindex="-1">9. Orca 1.4.243/244 ships host changes with explicit live-process boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-orca-1-4-243-244-ships-host-changes-with-explicit-live-process-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Orca 1.4.243/244 ships host changes with explicit live-process boundaries&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 1.4.243 uses a fresh terminal host for new terminals while old terminals stay attached to their previous host. Version 1.4.244 reuses 243&#39;s host and some terminal fixes require its restart. Version 243 continues a terminal-agent conversation in Chat after CLI exit and controls heartbeats for newly dispatched workers; 244 limits multi-agent Codex history growth and improves WSL/remote-file recovery. iOS setup now points to the App Store, Android remains APK and Chrome browser extensions remain experimental/off by default.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; keep second-circle priority with delivery/mobile/worker-lifecycle attention. Pending lead: an updated app does not replace every live process. Interaction: old-host status, worker configuration generations and remote version negotiation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/releases/tag/v1.4.243" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/releases/tag/v1.4.244" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_10-orca-pins-plugin-consent-to-bytes-alpha-merge-follows-the-stable-release" tabindex="-1">10. Orca pins plugin consent to bytes; Alpha merge follows the stable release <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-orca-pins-plugin-consent-to-bytes-alpha-merge-follows-the-stable-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Orca pins plugin consent to bytes; Alpha merge follows the stable release&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：GROUNDWORK IN 1.4.244, PLUGINS OFF BY DEFAULT; #35470 MERGED LATER</strong></p><p>Version 1.4.244 groundwork includes #30636 no-checkout downloads/exact catalog pins and #30637 content-hashed worker/panel consent. Each start/crash restart rechecks bytes and approval without a cached result; updated running workers restart through that gate. Reused already-running workers are not rehashed and local development folders are not byte-bound. Full Plugin Alpha #35470 merged at 03:58 UTC after 244&#39;s 03:55 release, so complete Alpha delivery in that release is not claimed.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; increase second-circle plugin-trust attention. Pending lead: download, install, consent, startup and updates have distinct authority. Interaction: development exceptions, running-code changes and crash-restart TOCTOU.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/30636" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/30637" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/35470" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_11-beads-permanent-tombstones-prevent-late-retries-from-resurrecting-retired-operations" tabindex="-1">11. Beads permanent tombstones prevent late retries from resurrecting retired operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#_11-beads-permanent-tombstones-prevent-late-retries-from-resurrecting-retired-operations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11. Beads permanent tombstones prevent late retries from resurrecting retired operations&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #7931/#7902/#7983; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#7931 atomically tombstones (id, closed_at) during explicitly retaining sweeps. Later create/rename paths return 410 idempotency_expired, imports skip/report and generators avoid retired IDs. Ordinary prune/purge/delete/gc do not retire IDs, so duplicate prevention is not universal. #7902 requires proof a localhost Dolt server serves the workspace directory and preserves remote data on reset. #7983 checks dependency deletion before copying a migration and aligns dry-run/real deletion sets to prevent half-moves.</p><p>High learning value, medium competitive risk; retain main-radar recovery/storage monitoring. Pending FCoP lead: retention expiry must not recycle an idempotency key into new work; localhost is not data identity. Interaction: tombstone growth/privacy deletion and preflight-to-commit races.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/7931" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/7902" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/7983" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_12-dspy-main-migrates-to-request-response-with-optional-litellm-compatibility" tabindex="-1">12. DSPy main migrates to Request/Response with optional LiteLLM compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-dspy-main-migrates-to-request-response-with-optional-litellm-compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12. DSPy main migrates to Request/Response with optional LiteLLM compatibility&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：SIX-PR STACK MERGED; RELEASE/PARITY NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>The six-PR stack including #10652 unifies adapters/custom engines around Request→Response, removes legacy messages=/forward/aforward integration and regenerates old caches rather than migrating them; string convenience calls still return lists. Final #10719 makes LiteLLM optional while retaining compatibility engines, selected before inference. Provider failures never switch backends. Native Chat audio/files, Anthropic citation fidelity, some provider parity and repeated-candidate billing remain open gates; merged main is not a verified 3.5 release or equivalent migration.</p><p>Pending lead: canonical contracts can align accounting/cache/history without establishing provider parity. Interaction: billed failed attempts, candidate call counts, endpoint/credential preservation and cache invalidation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10652" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10719" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10708" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_13-phoenix-and-vibe-kanban-align-declared-permissions-with-executor-paths" tabindex="-1">13. Phoenix and Vibe Kanban align declared permissions with executor paths <a class="header-anchor" href="#_13-phoenix-and-vibe-kanban-align-declared-permissions-with-executor-paths" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13. Phoenix and Vibe Kanban align declared permissions with executor paths&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #15828 / #3487; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>Phoenix #15828 keeps subagents read-only even when the main run bypasses edit approval, sanitizes MCP tools/list failures to exception classes before graceful degradation and clears deployment-scoped browser PATs on logout. Vibe Kanban #3487 routes Copilot through ExecutorApprovalBridge: previously Supervised disabled allow_all_tools but the executor still fell through to NoopApprovalService and silently approved requests. Release inclusion is unverified for both.</p><p>Pending lead: UI policy, backend flags and executor bridges must agree; checking settings alone is insufficient. Interaction: executor approval conformance, logout credential cleanup and degraded privileges. Vibe has high learning value but low competitive risk; retain the sunset historical/low-frequency classification without inferring commercial revival.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/pull/15828" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/BloopAI/vibe-kanban/pull/3487" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_14-uniemployee-0-24-0-ships-continuous-rss-collection-source-versions-and-recovery" tabindex="-1">14. UniEmployee 0.24.0 ships continuous RSS collection, source versions and recovery <a class="header-anchor" href="#_14-uniemployee-0-24-0-ships-continuous-rss-collection-source-versions-and-recovery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;14. UniEmployee 0.24.0 ships continuous RSS collection, source versions and recovery&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 0.24.0 adds RSS source management, import preview, per-source trials, continuous batches, original-source versions, unverified-material pages and startup recovery. Sources require explicit administrator import/configuration and tables initialize idempotently. Editable PPT generation/preview depends on a deployment runtime; without it the skill delivers content, not a complete deck. RAGFlow unavailability is explicit while other evidence remains usable. Test success is an upstream release claim; no local installation was performed.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; increase second-circle vertical-research monitoring. Pending lead: collection batches, source versions, unverified material and generated deliverables need separate states. Interaction: recovery deduplication, source authority and degraded PPT delivery contracts.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/zj-unicom-ai/UniEmployee/releases/tag/v0.24.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_15-mcp-documents-legacy-initialize-extension-negotiation-without-schema-changes" tabindex="-1">15. MCP documents legacy initialize extension negotiation without schema changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_15-mcp-documents-legacy-initialize-extension-negotiation-without-schema-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;15. MCP documents legacy initialize extension negotiation without schema changes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #3364; DOCUMENTATION ONLY</strong></p><p>#3364 clarifies extensions in initialize capabilities on initialization-based versions such as 2025-11-25, distinct from per-request capabilities in 2026-07-28 onward. Older published schemas omit the field and do not require it for conformance. Implementations must check SDK field preservation and individual-extension compatibility and degrade if missing. The upstream 9/10 SDK-main audit dates to October 27 and does not establish support across published packages or every extension.</p><p>Pending lead: core protocol version, SDK capability preservation and extension support are distinct. Interaction: bidirectional legacy tests, visibility when validators drop fields and per-extension Apps/Skills acceptance.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3364" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/extensions/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_16-langgraph-sdk-backs-off-reconnects-and-warns-about-cursorless-gaps" tabindex="-1">16. LangGraph SDK backs off reconnects and warns about cursorless gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#_16-langgraph-sdk-backs-off-reconnects-and-warns-about-cursorless-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16. LangGraph SDK backs off reconnects and warns about cursorless gaps&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #9481/#9482/#9477; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#9481 applies bounded five-attempt reconnect backoff to interrupted wait/join/cancel(wait=True) responses on 429/5xx/transport failures; initial-request failures and other statuses still raise. #9482 warns on reconnect without Last-Event-ID: future events continue but disconnected-period events cannot be replayed. #9477 preserves SSE empty data/CR framing and decoded cursors across chunks without claiming exhaustive conformance.</p><p>Pending FCoP lead: recovered connectivity is not complete trace coverage; warnings should enter evidence. Interaction: gap-aware recovery judgments, caller cursor trust and cross-client replay consistency.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/pull/9481" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/pull/9482" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/pull/9477" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_17-google-adk-rejects-truncated-claude-calls-and-ambiguous-url-authorities" tabindex="-1">17. Google ADK rejects truncated Claude calls and ambiguous URL authorities <a class="header-anchor" href="#_17-google-adk-rejects-truncated-claude-calls-and-ambiguous-url-authorities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;17. Google ADK rejects truncated Claude calls and ambiguous URL authorities&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：COMMITTED ON MAIN; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>Commit b9d2bc3 (#7558) returns MAX_TOKENS with content=None when Claude tool_use is truncated by max_tokens/model_context_window_exceeded, preventing incomplete calls and covering tool-backed structured output. Commit 4d0cd98 rejects model-supplied authorities containing backslashes, fixing a proxy/parser discrepancy between the validated host and the actual HTTP destination across load_web_page, RestApiTool and computer use. These are upstream code facts, not local vulnerability reproductions.</p><p>Pending lead: complete parsing and actual destinations must be established before effects. Interaction: proxy/redirect/parser consistency and preserving call identity when retrying truncation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/b9d2bc3d95487ecc4a437b813f9b8fa634e1be54" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/4d0cd985faedb3c0d9e3579077de327a6ef86a52" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_18-ag2-fixes-link-deletion-and-skill-executable-interpreter-handling" tabindex="-1">18. AG2 fixes link deletion and skill executable/interpreter handling <a class="header-anchor" href="#_18-ag2-fixes-link-deletion-and-skill-executable-interpreter-handling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;18. AG2 fixes link deletion and skill executable/interpreter handling&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #3479/#3433/#3511; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#3479 removes symlinks/Windows junctions without deleting targets, refuses recursive deletion of the base itself and lists links without traversing external contents. #3433 restores executable bits for regular archive files but not setuid/setgid so helpers can run. #3511 uses sys.executable for .py in the default Windows LocalRuntime without sending host interpreter paths to remote sandboxes. Upstream includes real-script before/after tests; no CodeFlowMu reproduction was performed.</p><p>Pending lead: path authority distinguishes link location from target, and installation permissions/execution environments are contracts. Interaction: junction/parent-link boundaries, archive privilege restrictions and Windows venv/remote isolation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3479" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3433" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3511" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" tabindex="-1">Cross-project synthesis and disposition <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project synthesis and disposition&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Claims and actual state:</strong> claimcheck receipts/probes, Concordat compliance, V³AST capability twins and Paperclip receipt reconciliation establish different facts, not one interchangeable success score.</li><li><strong>Owners and approval objects:</strong> Codex chat leases, Agents JS preparation owners and Canvas/Orca revision/content consent preserve authority across reuse and resume.</li><li><strong>Recovery is not completeness:</strong> cursorless LangGraph warnings, Paperclip resynchronization and tolerant OpenHands log loading must expose gaps to verification.</li><li><strong>Retention/deletion affects execution:</strong> Beads tombstones constrain late retries, AG2 unlinks entries without deleting targets and migration dry-runs must match real deletion sets.</li><li><strong>Release is not activation:</strong> Orca live hosts/default-off plugins/post-release Alpha, an OPEN Canvas companion and DSPy parity/billing gates retain separate states.</li></ul><p>Disposition is collection, classification and reporting only. Upstream experiments/numbers retain their original scope and are not local results. Local experiments: 0; articles: 0; formal development-review projects: 0; external comments/messages: 0. Interaction topics are recorded without assignments.</p><h2 id="dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" tabindex="-1">Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Latest-main Registry was read first with the connected GitHub tool: 47 researchers, blob 8d97df65269aa2b4accd1366d1b71319f99c6efd. People/repositories remain many-to-many. The six governance/verification researchers retain documented boundaries; no separate meaningful repository change was confirmed for them. bulbasaur-skill-cli is not AGENTSAFE&#39;s official artifact. Original papers establish the authors of Concordat/V³AST/SciΠvot; Zhao&#39;s additions are curation and a writing handoff. Aegis summarizes existing deployment/evaluation evidence here rather than establishing a new experiment.</p><p>The 88 targets include Registry sources, explicit technical supplements and 32 competitors. There are 904 commit records across 30 changed targets; 57 accessible zero-increment targets receive default-head checks. Release, commit and updated PR/issue checks lead to primary document/code reads for selected changes; Discussions, every architecture document and every license are not exhaustively reviewed. Superset remains the monitored name and uses verified ParadevAI/Paradev repository ID 1080370450 without changing the Registry.</p><p>Explicit gaps: Codex, Claude Code and Orca PR/issue feeds remain partial after 12 pages. TSA-AI-Workforce returns actual 404s on all three feeds; deletion/private status is not inferred. Codex&#39;s large release listing failed transport; latest-three/latest-stable recovery succeeds and stable remains 0.165.0. Most release lists cover the latest ten rather than all history. Dynamic pagination stores record/unique counts. Only completed successful source queries advance their watermarks; no global full-coverage watermark exists.</p><p>The second source type is separate: 12 grouped public-person/discussion searches for the current 47 people plus targeted searches of Registry-confirmed X URLs, with original papers/project pages and official Cursor/Audit Commons/Paradev/MCP pages opened. No reliably dated new personal post is counted; this does not exhaust X. Same-named third-party accounts are not guessed. Cursor&#39;s visible October 20 material is historical. Audit Commons now exposes October 27 articles predating this window; they remain backfill leads, not October 29 launches. Newly curated papers were originally submitted October 27, 26 and 15. GitHub primary events independently support engineering facts; two GitHub links are not two source types.</p><h2 id="github-archive-and-website-delivery" tabindex="-1">GitHub archive and website delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-archive-and-website-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub archive and website delivery&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both languages retain the same 18 groups, states, 42 evidence links and judgments. Only the two dated reports and original indexes are written through connected GitHub tools, retaining structure, unique descending dates and fixed entry points. Existing-site builds and non-force gh-pages publication preserve layout, public snapshots, translation caches and other published content. Latest-main/publication concurrency checks, four-file readback, matching Pages completion and bilingual live click acceptance are stored in local receipts; this body does not pre-claim online success. Research Runtime business facts, scheduling, article candidates and lifecycle states are untouched.</p>',103)])])}const g=a(i,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
